    "https://www.boisestate.edu/jobs/faculty-recruitment/": {
      "title": "Boise Global Navigation",
      "description": "Faculty Recruitment - Careers at Boise State Skip to main content Careers at Boise State Home Search Menu Boise Global Navigation Apply Visit Give Menu Ready to take the next step? Plan your virtual visit. Search Search All Boise State Careers at Boise State Only Careers at Boise State Current Opportunities Faculty Recruitment Contact HR About Boise State Human Resources Home Benefits Work/Life Balance More section menu items Careers at Boise State Faculty Recruitment Faculty Recruitment The Recruitment Process: 1) Strategic planning, 2) Search Preparation, 3) Advertise and Outreach, 4) Screening and Evaluation, 5) Initial Interview, 6) On Campus Interview, 7) Verbal and Written Offer, 8) Onboarding In This Section: PageUp Standard Employment Dates Search Committee Training Faculty Position Action Request Form Considerations for a Fair and Thoughtful Search Boise State Faculty Careers Position Eligibility for Visa Sponsorship Form Thank you for your commitment to hiring exceptional faculty, and providing prospective faculty candidates with an outstanding experience with Boise State University. The work that you are doing as part of an active search committee is integral to the ongoing success of our campus as a whole, and we appreciate the time and effort you and your team contribute. This site offers an outline of the process, resources and tools to help navigate the complex faculty hiring process. Our team is available to advise department chairs and search committees on recruitment strategies and equitable search practices, and to respond to any questions that may arise during the recruitment process. For questions, contact the HR Faculty Recruiting Team at FacultyRecruiting@boisestate.edu or (208) 426-1657. “Recruiting and retaining talented, high-quality faculty into our programs at Boise State is a top priority. Faculty play a vitally important role in the pursuit of Boise State’s goals within our strategic plan; they support the success of our students, drive innovation both in the classroom and in research and creative activity, and help build partnerships with our surrounding communities in the Treasure Valley and the state of Idaho.” Zeynep K. Hansen, Ph.D., Interim Provost and Vice President for Academic Affairs 8 Process Stages Stage 1 – Strategic Planning Stage 2 – Search Preparation Stage 3 – Advertise and Outreach Stage 4 – Screening and Evaluation Stage 5 –  Initial Interview Stage 6 – On Campus Interview Stage 7 – Verbal and Written Offer Stage 8 – Onboarding Strategic Planning This initial phase of the faculty hiring process typically involves department/unit leaders, college deans, business managers and department recruitment coordinators, along with input and approval from the Office of the Provost. When considering faculty positions, Human Resources (HR) encourages the hiring department to pause and consider the items in this section before replacing the same faculty or research line. A successful faculty hire builds for the future of the department. The Office of the Provost or the Faculty Recruiter are available to engage in strategic planning discussions either from a long-term department planning perspective or in envisioning a specific position or role. Review of departmental needs: Where is the discipline or field of study headed? What specific areas of focus need to be strengthened? How does student demand for courses and/or research opportunities impact the needs of the department? Are there program growth or retraction considerations? Department, College or University initiatives that could support or be supported by this role. School/College reviews departmental proposals: The process of identifying priorities may be school/college specific. Please confirm the requirements and steps for approval with the dean or business manager in your college. Note: Typically in June a communication will be sent to all deans from the Office of the Provost requesting the faculty recruitment plans for the upcoming academic year. These requests are then reviewed and discussed with the Provost and Dean directly. Provost approval of the plan must be received before recruiting for positions. Clinical or Teaching Faculty and Lecturers: Clinical or teaching faculty and lecturer lines are approved at the college dean level for all colleges. These should also be noted on the faculty hiring plans submitted to the Provost. Tenure Track Positions: Each school/college approves tenure track positions internally, and then submits a recruitment plan to the Provost for approval. The Provost will review requests and may approve or deny them based on their evaluation of position rank, college resources, and overall academic strategy. Adjunct Faculty Positions: While the hiring of adjunct faculty does not need dean or provost approval, it does need to adhere to university policies and practices of hiring. Read more about university policies and procedures in Stage 2 of this web page. Department/unit leaders: After receiving Provost and dean approval, reach out to the Human Resources Faculty Recruiting team to discuss best practices for launching a successful faculty search, including goals, timing, and other considerations for the approved search(es). Questions related to faculty salaries and budgets can typically be answered by the School/College business manager or Dean’s office. The business manager in the Office of the Provost is also a resource for these questions. Adjunct hiring is handled via Letter of Appointment (LOA) actions in Bronco Hub. More information on adjunct faculty pay and the hiring process for adjunct faculty can be found here . Questions related to the adjunct hiring/LOA process can be directed to HRProcessor@boisestate.edu . Note: HR is able to support the advertising (posting to the career site and/or HigherEd Jobs) of open adjunct positions either on a limited need or ongoing basis. To discuss a department’s adjunct needs, please reach out to the Human Resources Faculty Recruiting Team. Resources: Word Cloud Strategic Planning Activity Visiting or limited duration faculty guide Search Preparation This section is arguably the most important stage of the entire process, as it requires work from the department/unit leader in creating a clear vision for the position, establishing a well-prepared and diverse search committee, and partnering with Human Resources (HR) to build a marketing/search plan. The department/unit leader is also responsible for providing the committee with a clear charge which clarifies the needs of the department as it relates to a specific position and search. Best practice workflow: Department/unit leader forms a search committee. Department/unit leader selects a search committee chair and charges them with a goal. Committee commits to adhering to all university hiring policies and practices, including but not limited to non-discrimination and anti-harassment ; Equal Opportunity statement and Veteran’s preference ; and nepotism . Committee crafts a position description template , in light of the committee’s charge, partnering with the HR Faculty Recruiter. Please review these resources related to drafting a clear and candidate focused position description, with consideration of the minimum qualifications for each position type . Department Recruiting Coordinator (DRC) submits requisition in PageUp. This will require approval from the department/unit leader, Office of the Provost and Human Resources Faculty Recruiting Team. Committee discusses and creates evaluation criteria and process for review of applicant files. See Stage 4 “Screening and Evaluation”. Once the position description has been drafted, the committee should create a long range scheduling plan with calendar appointments for recruiting, reviewing applications, meeting as committees, interviews, and final selections. This will avoid scheduling conflicts. Account for holidays, grading periods, and other times when it may be difficult to get faculty members’ attention or time. Finalize or update Search Timeline/Calendar and start planning for virtual interviews and on-campus visits. The HR Faculty Recruiting Team is available to partner with you and your department through any piece of this stage. Resources: Search Committee Training Course Clarification of roles in the Faculty Recruiting Process Faculty Search Committee Guidance Faculty Search Committee – Conflict of Interest Guidance Faculty Minimum Qualifications by role Faculty Position Action Request Form Faculty Position Description Writing Guide Faculty Position Description Template Faculty Job Boards with Pricing Options Request to Waive Recruitment Job Aid – PageUp – Create a Faculty Requisition Job Aid – Approving or declining a requisition Position Description to Evaluation Rubric AI prompt activity Word Cloud Strategic Planning Activity Visiting or limited duration faculty guide Faculty search timeline with gantt view Advertise and Outreach Search Committee: All members of the search committee are required to complete the search committee training course . As a baseline, all open, national-search faculty positions will be posted both to the Boise State Career site and to HigherEd Jobs. The minimum posting time for a national search is five business days, but Human Resources (HR) recommends at least 30 days for a thorough search and to allow candidates the necessary time to apply. The HR Faculty Recruiting team will work to locate and reach out to sources for nominations and directly to potential candidates and will also post to a variety of other sites including, but not limited to: LinkedIn APLU SREB (Southern Region Education Board) Note: Additional advertising on paid job boards will be processed through a 3rd party vendor, JobElephant. Billing for those will be sent directly to the department administrator for payment. Before any position is posted for applications, the department/unit leader and search committee, in partnership with the HR Faculty Recruiting team, drafts an initial search plan for the approved search. This search plan outlines active outreach efforts, including: Reaching out to other universities or organizations by the department/unit leader or other colleagues for both nominators (potential sources for candidates) and nominees (potential candidates). Determining if there are listservs, blogs and other online venues relevant to this field/discipline where there may be benefits in posting the position. Confirming if there are any conferences that faculty are attending before or during the sourcing window where candidates can be scouted. Or where additional advertising may be beneficial. Reviewing award lists that can be searched for potential nominees. Looking at available venues for advertisements. Review recommended advertising options if shared by faculty recruiting team, or notify faculty recruiting team. Posting on social media (department and/or individual profiles) to feeds, industry groups, and more. People who are members of the hiring discipline are the best resource to locate high quality candidates and to develop relationships with nominators who can provide referrals to great potential candidates. It is recommended that all department faculty, not only those serving on the committee be included in this sourcing phase of the process. Search Committee Chair: If it wasn’t established alongside the drafting of the position description, this posting period is a great time to meet as a committee and determine how to evaluate candidates who end up in your candidate pool. Preparing for how candidates will be reviewed and evaluated will speed up the review process when the window for applications closes. This is also a great time to start planning your on-campus visits, getting meeting spaces setup and scheduling blocks of time with the committee and department and key stakeholders or decision makers to ensure maximum availability. Resources: Internal Candidates – Committee Guidance Internal Candidates – Candidate Guidance Candidate evaluation tools and templates Screening and Evaluation Now that the application window has closed, or the initial review date has arrived, it is time to evaluate candidates and select those who will proceed to an initial interview. The committee should use the evaluation tool or screening rubric that was ideally developed alongside the position description to support an intentional process to review candidates individually and as a committee. There is a natural tension at this step of the process between speed/efficiency and thoroughness. The primary goal in this stage is evaluate each candidate fairly, based intentionally on the criteria in the job description, and to make an initial assessment of candidacy. Rushing through the process can create a scenario in which committee members miss potential candidate strengths or evaluate candidates based on superficial features.  At the same time, taking too much time in this stage can cause the process to draw out, which can be frustrating and  risk losing candidates to other institutions. Building evaluation tools: If evaluation tools were not created in the earlier stages, start by defining clear criteria around the following qualifications: Scholarly impact (avoid shortcuts such as substituting quantity of publications or journal impact factors for the actual evaluation of the impact of the work). Scholarly productivity and potential for continued contributions and impact. Research/creative activities funding and future potential for such funding. Ability and potential to attract and mentor graduate students. Ability and potential to teach and supervise undergraduates. Ability and potential to successfully attract, teach, and advise a diversity of students. Commitment to collaboration with colleagues and community partners. Commitment to the university and department’s strategic plan. Investing positive contributions to the department’s climate. Being a conscientious department citizen. Candidate evaluation tools can vary by search but should be consistently applied to every candidate, tied to the posted position description requirements, and clear about committee scoring and candidate qualification criteria. Evaluation may be completed directly in PageUp, where committee members can review candidate materials, add file notes, and assign basic scores. Department Recruitment Coordinators (DRCs) may also download applicant files for committee review; however, as a best practice, downloaded materials should only be stored in a secure shared drive with limited access granted solely to search committee members. Evaluation tools can range from a rubric-based scoring sheet to a more complex and customized scoring spreadsheet. While evaluation methods are determined by the committee, the process must remain clear, measurable, documented for decision-making purposes, and consistently applied to all candidates by all committee members. For open-rank searches, it is a best practice to establish separate evaluation criteria for each rank level (for example, assistant professor versus full professor). HR Note: If the committee opts to download applicant files for review outside of the PageUp system, please note that there are often flags related to visa sponsorship, veterans status, and current employment status that will not transfer to a packet download. Department Recruitment Coordinators (DRCs) and Search Committee Chairs have a responsibility to ensure that these flags are taken into account for the selected interview pool. Reviewing candidate files: Ideally all committee members review all applications. At minimum, all applications should be reviewed by more than one person. If there are hundreds of applicants, the search chair should assign subsets to each committee member and should vary the pairings of reviewers to avoid creating “mini-committees of two.” Ensure all candidate files are thoroughly reviewed. As the committee identifies strong candidates, also look for reasons to keep candidates under consideration, rather than exclude them, to ensure all candidates are fully considered. If the search was set up with an ‘open until filled’ status with a review date, keep in mind that candidate files received need to be reviewed and evaluated with the same process as the initial pool. In some cases this is a handful of additional applicants to review, and in some it may be quite a few. If you have a larger second group of candidates we suggest going with a ‘Pool A’ and ‘Pool B’ mentality and evaluating all applicants completely. Please notify the Faculty Recruiting team once you feel you have identified a qualified pool and wish to close the posting. This is typically once you feel comfortable with the quality of the pool at review, when you find you are unable to thoroughly review applications coming in to the same standard, or once finalists are identified to come to campus. Candidate interview selection: Once the committee reaches some form of agreement on the candidates to invite for an initial interview, the DRC will need to mark those candidates as ‘To be interviewed’ in PageUp so that Human Resources (HR) can review the pool. In most cases, this is a list of 8 to 12 candidates (number can vary by pool). If the position is remote-eligible, HR and the General Counsel office will review the list of candidates to ensure the committee has the information needed as it relates to limitations and costs of hiring candidates in other locations. Candidates not selected or determined to not be moving forward should be marked as ‘Search Committee Review Unsuccessful’. It is possible you may have some candidates not updated to either ‘To Be Interviewed’ or ‘Search Committee Review Unsuccessful’ if you are holding them in reserve. If that is the case, leave them as ‘Search Committee Review’ status until a final decision has been made on their candidacy. Resources: Position Eligibility for Visa Sponsorship Form Candidate evaluation tools and templates Faculty Interview Questions Interview topics to avoid Best Practices for Virtual Interviewing Consistency and Fairness in Online Interviews PageUp Icon Clarification Job Aid – PageUp Candidate Review Faculty Candidate Application Statuses Faculty Candidate PageUp System Notification Messages Internal Candidates – Committee Guidance Internal Candidates – Candidate Guidance Selecting candidates to be interviewed Initial Interview Candidate interviews: Once candidate selections are made, the committee can begin inviting candidates for an initial interview. This interview is generally conducted virtually, utilizing Zoom or Google Meet. Some things to consider to prepare for interviews: All interviews are a two-way street. Search committee members are determining if a candidate is a good fit for the position and candidates are determining if Boise State is a place where they can see themselves being successful. Maintain that mindset when communicating and interacting with candidates. As a committee, determine what interview questions to ask . These questions should be consistent and asked of each candidate. It also helps to assign questions to committee members in advance. Determine which questions are most critical given the time allotted for the interview. In a normal 45 minute interview (with 15 minutes for committee discussion to follow), you can ask 5-7 strong questions. This may mean asking fewer questions, determining which questions are most critical, and which might be extracted from candidate submissions. To open the virtual interview, the search chair should introduce themselves and the committee and give an overview of the agenda. ALWAYS allow a few minutes at the end of the interview for the candidate to ask questions of the committee. This time is valuable from a candidate experience perspective and can also give the committee additional insight into the candidate. The timing and format of the interview with each candidate should be as consistent as possible (e.g., giving some candidates more time or asking different follow-up questions can mean that at this stage not every candidate has the same opportunity to demonstrate what they have to offer). It can be useful to have a “run of show” and timekeeper to maintain consistency. In addition to determining which questions to ask, please review the list of topics to avoid in an interview . Take notes during the interview to allow for clear reflection on candidates later in the process. Best Practice Recommendation: Post the questions asked in the chat function of your virtual meeting room to help candidates ensure they understood the question and answered completely. This is especially helpful if the questions are multi-part. Selecting on-campus interviews: It is strongly recommended that all candidates invited for campus visits be submitted for export control review . This ensures compliance with federal regulations. A campus visit involving an individual from or educated in an embargoed , sanctioned , or prohibited country, destination, or organization, or an individual who generally poses significant risk, must be approved by the export control office in advance of scheduling the finalist on-campus interviews. At the conclusion of the initial interviews, each committee member should review their notes and the rating system to determine who they would like to see invited for a final on-campus interview. Candidates not selected to move forward to an on-campus interview should be updated to ‘Interview 1 unsuccessful’ and notified via PageUp. Candidates held in reserve here, should remain in ‘To Be Interviewed’ status until a final decision has been made. Notifying reserve or not-selected candidates: It is recommended that candidates not being selected for interviews be notified of this decision as soon as possible. Transparency and clear communication is associated with a positive candidate experience, even when a candidate is not selected. Please review these attached guides which outline the different candidate statuses available , what notification messages are attached to each, and best practice recommendations for reserve candidates. Provost Best Practice: Please note that internal candidates should receive personal notification (by phone or email) ahead of sending the system generated notification. Candidate Materials and Documentation: All candidate materials are to remain confidential and should be utilized only by the designated search committee. Sharing of materials or discussion of specific candidates outside of the committee is a violation of university policy and candidate trust. This confidentiality extends in perpetuity and should be maintained even after the conclusion of the search. At the end of every search, Human Resources will also reach out to gather materials related to the selection process. This will include things like: evaluation rubrics/scoring sheets, interview questions and notes, committee surveys/notes, reference check notes and other documentation related to the search process etc. Best practice recommendation is to store search and interview materials in a shared folder that the department DRC can share with HR at the conclusion of the search. Resources: Position Eligibility for Visa Sponsorship Form Faculty Interview Questions Interview topics to avoid Best Practices for Virtual Interviewing Consistency and Fairness in Online Interviews Faculty Candidate Application Statuses Faculty Candidate PageUp System Notification Messages Export Control and Faculty Hiring Quick Guide Job Aid – External Interactions Submission Internal Candidates – Committee Guidance Internal Candidates – Candidate Guidance Selecting candidates to be interviewed On Campus Interview It is easy to think of the on-campus visit as just another interview stage, but in reality, this is where the focus of a search should shift from selection to recruitment. Each candidate who comes to campus should leave with a favorable impression of Boise State, regardless of whether a candidate is chosen as the finalist. The on-campus visit allows finalists to showcase their professional and scholarly pursuits, and is also an opportunity for the unit to welcome finalists into a new community. Remember to consider a candidate’s experience when building out interview plans. The visits, including interviews, presentations, and preparation time should be carefully planned to ensure that all candidates receive the same treatment and are evaluated in the same manner. It is best practice to interview internal candidates first from a finalist pool. This practice avoids internal candidates having a potential advantage of firsthand information about other candidates’ final interviews. The Search Chair (or designated faculty ambassador) should check with the candidates throughout the visit to ensure interviews are proceeding in a positive manner and to address any potential issues. All visiting candidates should have direct contact information for the search chair as well as anyone who will be picking them up or dropping them off at interview activities. If there are finalists who require visa sponsorship (as flagged in PageUp), and the search chair has not yet connected with the Center for Global Engagement office, please do so immediately. Best practices for executing a successful on-campus search: Identify primary staff support to coordinate all necessary documentation, travel arrangements, and reimbursements. Develop an agenda for the visit which includes (when relevant) the names of individuals who will be helping candidates move from one location to another, and provides the cell phone numbers of those individuals. There are a variety of different resources on campus that it may be valuable to connect your candidates with on their visit. This could include things like HR Benefits, Center for Teaching and Learning (CTL), Center for Research and Creative Activity, eCampus, Global Engagement, etc. The Faculty Recruiting Team is happy to consult on what options are available around campus, what might be a good fit for your candidates, and how to best connect with those areas. Assemble an information packet to share with each candidate with a detailed schedule identifying the name and affiliation of each person they will meet. Photographs, websites, and a map make it easier for candidates to orient themselves before arriving. Involve candidates in interactions with faculty and students in multiple venues, including talks, individual and group meetings, and meals. Gather relevant materials from around campus for a ‘hotel packet’ for each candidate. This can include HR benefits information, campus brochures, faculty development resources, employee resource groups, or research tools available on campus. Also consider including Information about the City of Boise, a variety of restaurant menu options, and a list of local attractions to showcase what the Treasure Valley has to offer a prospective candidate. The Faculty Recruiting Team is available to help assemble information packets, or guide you in putting it together. If a job talk is expected, notify each candidate, providing clear guidance about what is expected. For example, indicate the anticipated length of the talk and clarify whether the unit is interested in a specific research topic or a broad overview of the candidate’s research program and future research plans. If the candidate is asked to do a teaching demonstration, clarify what is expected and who the audience will be. Faculty in the department should also know what the candidate has been asked to prepare and what instructions were given for the candidate’s public presentation. Schedule candidate interviews with the unit administrator (chair, director, or dean) Ensure that all faculty and community members interacting with candidates are reminded about professional standards of conduct and legal limitations for questions. Review Interview Topics to Avoid . All committee members should attend all job talks/presentations in person, or at minimum watch recordings of those sessions. Ideally, all voting members of the department would also attend or view all job talks/presentations. Without consistent attendance, committee members and others may inadvertently give more weight to candidates whose talks they attended. Best Practice Recommendation: Add committee meetings and candidate presentations to committee member calendars as early as possible. Enlist graduate and undergraduate students to attend job talks/presentations to ensure they are well attended. The committee should encourage students to ask questions (and solicit their feedback). If the search or hiring committee has committed to providing follow-up information to the candidate, ensure it is delivered promptly. Immediately following an on-campus visit, the Search Chair should email everyone who interacted with the candidate, and ask for feedback, on a standardized form. These sample forms can be used for initial candidate review as well as this broader collection of feedback on job talks, meetings, and interviews. Creating a google form is a great method to collect feedback. Once all campus interviews have been completed, hold a search committee meeting to document the strengths and areas of concern for each finalist. The committee will typically draft a statement for the department/unit leader that outlines their judgements. This should relate directly to the position description, evaluation criteria, and feedback received. If the department chair/hiring decision maker requests that the committee select a final candidate, a vote may be conducted. Final decision making authority typically rests with the department chair/unit leader in consultation with the dean and provost. Resources: Position Eligibility for Visa Sponsorship Form Embargoed, Sanctioned, or Prohibited Destinations, Organizations, or Individuals Visit Expense Information (Meals, Hotels, Transportation) Candidate Evaluation Tools and Templates Faculty Interview Questions Interview topics to avoid Faculty Candidate Application Statuses Faculty Candidate PageUp System Notification Messages Export Control and Faculty Hiring Quick Guide Job Aid – External Interactions Submission Internal Candidates – Committee Guidance Internal Candidates – Candidate Guidance Sample visit agenda templates Verbal and Written Offer Next Steps After Interviews: After interviews are complete and feedback has been gathered from faculty and department stakeholders, it’s time to check references for final candidates. This is typically done via phone call and many committees utilize this sample reference check document . It is possible to conduct reference checks via the PageUp system (which sends the listed references an email form to complete). Both the reference check document and PageUp form are built to ask similar, faculty focused questions. . Reference Check notes: HR Best Practice: Be sure to notify candidates that you are starting to contact references BEFORE reaching out to any listed references. General rule is to conduct three reference checks. If references listed by the candidate are not responsive, make at least two communication attempts for each listed reference to ensure due diligence. Three references are generally gathered as part of the candidate application (in PageUp). HR Best Practice: Limit reference calls to names that were given by or approved by the candidate. If you feel additional references are needed, please discuss directly with the candidate who may be able to provide additional references or context for why they would not want you to call certain references. In the interest of candidate experience and professional courtesy, calling off-list references should be used sparingly if at all. Calling references not approved by the candidate opens the candidate up to risks in their current position, can damage their current working relationships, and the value of information gathered may not outweigh the potential ramifications. Be sure to ask references about skills and/or abilities relevant to the position. DO NOT ask about anything related to the person’s age, race, gender or personal life. Apply the same ‘What not to ask rules’ as the interview process. If a reference is poor, try to understand the performance issue and potential causes by asking questions. Consider how these issues might continue in the new position. If a final candidate requires visa sponsorship, be sure to update the Center for Global Engagement office on the status of the search. Once final notes have been compiled by the committee and shared with the department/unit leader or hiring manager, the decision is confirmed by the department/unit leader and Dean. Export Control Review Before making a verbal offer for any faculty position (including Tenure-Track, Clinical, Teaching, Lecturer, or Lab Instructor roles), the finalist’s CV must be submitted to the export control team for review by completing an External Interaction in InfoEd . If export control review was completed ahead of on-campus visits, it does not need to be resubmitted. This review must be completed before extending any verbal offer. Provost Approval for All Faculty Offers In addition to export control review, all faculty offers require Provost approval. The dean (or designee) must submit a selection statement, confirmation of export control review, and the candidate’s CV to the Provost (cc Kristen DeBoer) before any verbal offer is made. After approval to make the offer is received, the typical first step is for the department/unit leader or dean to contact the finalist and make a verbal offer. This verbal offer stage will typically involve candidate negotiations related to salary, pre-tenure course reductions, credit for prior service toward tenure , startup funds, and graduate assistantships. All candidate agreements should be communicated to Human Resources (HR) and the Faculty Recruiter to ensure it is included in the final offer letter. Once a verbal offer is accepted, information related to salary, start up funds, space allocation, moving expenses, tenure and promotion credits should be relayed to the Department Recruitment Coordinator (DRC) for entry into the offer card in PageUp . Submission of the offer card will launch a background check and degree verification process in HR. HR will also draft an offer letter which will be sent electronically to the candidate after it is reviewed by the hiring manager, budget approver, and Provost. Be sure to communicate with HR all verbal negotiations with the candidate. When the candidate accepts the written offer (sent electronically), the candidate will receive new employee paperwork, and move to HR onboarding. Notify any unsuccessful candidates only after an offer has been accepted, but prior to public announcements about who has accepted the appointment. HR Best Practice: The department or search chair should contact finalists directly to let unsuccessful candidates know they were not selected, this applies to anyone who made a visit to campus. In addition, any internal candidates should receive a direct communication if they are not selected. All candidates should also be updated in PageUp so applications status is updated. There is a two-day delay on notification emails from the system, so system updates and finalist contacts can occur simultaneously. The HR team will contact the DRC (and possibly search chair) to gather final documentation related to the search. As a reminder, this includes evaluation rubrics and scoring sheets; interview questions and notes; committee surveys and notes; and reference check notes. If the committee has stored materials in a shared drive, that drive link can be shared directly with HR Faculty Recruiting. In the event of a failed, cancelled or unsuccessful search, candidate statuses should be updated via PageUp. It is again recommended that any internal candidates and/or finalists who came to campus receive a direct communication of the decision to close the search without an offer. In the event of reposting, please connect with the Faculty Recruiting team to build a strategy for the next search. The Provost office, as part of an effort to increase faculty retention will be gathering information from deans on any successful or unsuccessful offers made. The aim is to gather data to better understand why we are or are not being selected by our chosen candidates and to identify areas of improvement in the search and offer process. Deans and Department Chairs/Unit Leaders should be prepared to communicate details of both accepted and declined offers. Resources: Verbal Offer Guidance Position Eligibility for Visa Sponsorship Form Embargoed, Sanctioned, or Prohibited Destinations, Organizations, or Individuals Faculty Reference Check Template Job Aid – Send Reference Checks Job Aid – Review Reference Check Responses Faculty Candidate Application Statuses Faculty Candidate PageUp System Notification Messages Dual Career Assistance Program (Non-Faculty Spouse/Partner) Dual Career Policy (Faculty Spouse/Partner) Standard Employment Dates Job Aid – Gather Resources Job Aid – Offer Card Verbal Offer Negotiated Terms Export Control and Faculty Hiring Quick Guide Job Aid – External Interactions Submission Internal Candidates – Committee Guidance Internal Candidates – Candidate Guidance Onboarding Once a candidate has accepted an offer, it is time to start building an onboarding plan. Keep in mind that onboarding a new faculty member is as important as any part of the selection process. The best way to support a new faculty member is to help them build connections and integrate with their peers, colleagues and key individuals across campus. This will help them assimilate to their new career at Boise State, and increase employee engagement and retention. Onboarding is more than an initial setup and welcome to campus. Successful onboarding is at least a one year commitment. This responsibility lies primarily with the department/unit leader, but the entire unit should be involved and commit to creating a welcoming environment. The Faculty Recruiting Team is available to consult with leaders and support hiring best practices.. When a candidate accepts their electronic offer from Human Resources (HR), they will complete the IDBSU information form which will start the process of building an electronic employee profile in the HR and payroll systems. This will also launch HR onboarding emails to provide them with information on essential tasks, required paperwork and instructions for how to sign up for their employee benefits. Supervisors and department/unit leaders should utilize the supervisor onboarding checklist to support a successful onboarding process. It’s important to let new faculty know about New Faculty Orientation and the Faculty Mentoring Program . New Faculty Orientation is a great welcome to campus, and helps build a network with other incoming faculty. Please encourage incoming faculty to arrive in Boise in time for this orientation (typically the Monday/Tuesday before the first week of classes). If your department has a culture of faculty mentoring, connect the new faculty member with colleagues who can support their professional development. Think through what resources a new faculty member needs to be successful in research, teaching, service, and work-life balance. Share information about the program and course learning outcomes in your department; share syllabi for the courses the new faculty member will be teaching; provide information about how to order course materials, etc. Intentionally connect new employees with research-related resources and potential research partners both in the department and in other areas of the university (for example, the Office of Sponsored Programs, the Center for Research and Creative Activities). As you engage new colleagues, be aware that new employees can easily be overwhelmed by too much information given at once. Think best how to prioritize information and distribute it across the first semester and even the first year. A series of summer newsletters are sent to all new faculty ahead of their fall semester start date. These newsletters cover important information from HR and around campus, and include resources relevant to incoming faculty and a calendar of events that new faculty should be aware of. A condensed version of this newsletter is also sent in January for any incoming faculty starting work in the spring semester. Spring semester hires are welcome and eligible to attend the New Faculty Orientation offered the following fall. Generally speaking, you will make an offer to a faculty candidate in the spring for that person to start in late summer or fall of the following semester. This can be a lengthy period of time where there is little communication from Boise State. Be sure to intentionally stay in contact with your incoming faculty to keep them engaged, help with their transition and to reinforce that they have made the right decision to come to Boise State. It is also a good idea to notify your candidate of any pre-semester department retreats or other events for which the dates are known. Knowing these dates can be especially important for people timing their move to the Boise area. If dates are not known, letting new faculty know that dates for these events will be communicated as soon as they are known is helpful. Boise State offers several resources that can potentially help you support your candidate with things like relocation , housing , childcare , and spousal/partner career support . Complete the I-9 form: All new hires are required by law to complete an I-9 form on or before their first day of employment. This can be completed remotely if they are not in Boise on their contract date (July). The HR offices are open from 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. Monday through Friday. The I-9 form should be completed no later than close of business on the Friday before classes start. Questions related to I-9 forms can be directed to i9information@boisestate.edu or 208-426-1616. Resources: Global Engagement – Visa Assistance Relocation Guide Boise State Faculty Careers (Campus, Benefits, Resources) Children’s Center Priority Waitlist request form Employee Housing Assistance Program Dual Career Assistance Program Supervisor Onboarding Checklist Human Resources I-9 Information Page Human Resources Payroll information Human Resources Benefits Information Individual Development Plan Office of the Provost – Faculty Handbook Contacts and resources The HR Faculty Recruiting Team invites units to share their successful best practices and tools with us to improve recruitment and hiring success across campus. We will survey committee members at the close of each faculty search to monitor the needs of campus. Visit Expense Information Meals, Hotels, Transportation Visit Expense Information Meals, Hotels, Transportation Human Resources and Workforce Strategy Payroll, Benefits, etc. Human Resources and Workforce Strategy Payroll, Benefits, etc. Resources for International Faculty and Visiting Scholars Center for Global Engagement Resources for International Faculty and Visiting Scholars Center for Global Engagement Position Eligibility for Visa Sponsorship Form Position Eligibility for Visa Sponsorship Form Position Eligibility for Visa Sponsorship Form Relocation Expenses and Policies Relocation Expenses and Policies Relocation Expenses and Policies Related Policies Employment of Full-Time Benefit-Eligible Faculty Policy 4210 Employment of Full-Time Benefit-Eligible Faculty Policy 4210 Faculty Dual Career Program Policy 4240 Faculty Dual Career Program Policy 4240 Faculty Tenure and Promotion Faculty Tenure and Promotion Faculty Tenure and Promotion Clinical Faculty Promotion Policy 4490 Clinical Faculty Promotion Policy 4490 Research Faculty Promotion Policy 5010 Research Faculty Promotion Policy 5010 Search and Appointment of Academic Administrators Policy 4500 Search and Appointment of Academic Administrators Policy 4500 Meet the Faculty Recruiting Team Contact the faculty recruiting team at facultyrecruiting@boisestate.edu Search Kristen Martin Faculty Recruiter Ask Me About Developing a faculty recruiting strategy Search committee guidance Building a robust applicant pool Consultative support and search planning Call: (208) 426-1657 kristenmartin@boisestate.edu Ask Me About Developing a faculty recruiting strategy Search committee guidance Building a robust applicant pool Consultative support and search planning Andrea Burton Talent Acquisition Specialist Ask Me About Recruitment Coordination PageUp Assistance DRC Training Offer Cards and Letters Call: (208) 426-1659 aburton@boisestate.edu Ask Me About Recruitment Coordination PageUp Assistance DRC Training Offer Cards and Letters Careers at Boise State hr@boisestate.edu (208) 426-1616 960 S Broadway Ave., University Plaza, Ste. 100, Boise, ID 83725-1265 Secondary Navigation myBoiseState Safety, Security and Support Career Opportunities Boise State Public Radio Albertsons Library Transportation and Parking Partner with Us Morrison Center Alumni Bronco Shop and Bookstore University Administration ExtraMile Arena Broncos Logo Athletics Follow us on Facebook Follow us on Instagram Follow us on X Follow us on Youtube Follow us on LinkedIn Terms of Use Accessibility Privacy Classroom Concerns Policies Contact Boise State © 2026 All Rights Reserved\n\t\t\t\t\t\t Boise State University."
    },
    "https://www.boisestate.edu/jobs/worklife-balance/": {
      "title": "Boise Global Navigation",
      "description": "Work/Life Balance - Careers at Boise State Skip to main content Careers at Boise State Home Search Menu Boise Global Navigation Apply Visit Give Menu Ready to take the next step? Plan your virtual visit. Search Search All Boise State Careers at Boise State Only Careers at Boise State Current Opportunities Faculty Recruitment Contact HR About Boise State Human Resources Home Benefits Work/Life Balance More section menu items Careers at Boise State Work/Life Balance Work/Life Balance Boise State University encourages healthy lifestyles and provides a positive work environment that promotes a much-needed balance between home and work responsibilities. You have a variety of options at your disposal for putting more LIFE in your work/life balance. Enjoy Boise Discover a vibrant community where you can connect with dynamic people and enjoy a rich variety of arts and entertainment. Or take a quick day trip into the most spectacular wilderness playground in the world. Breathe, Play, Sweat or Swim Boise State Campus Recreation offers you a stunning world class facility that boasts the latest programs, equipment and amenities for all faculty, staff and students. Here you’ll be able to re-energize and replenish your body, mind and spirit. Caring for Yourself All Boise State faculty and staff are eligible to receive medical and wellness services at the Health Center where you’ll find a full range of primary care, urgent care and wellness services. Conveniently located on campus, board certified medical professionals and wellness experts are available to help address your health care needs. Time Away The University provides a substantial holiday and vacation package for all benefit-eligible faculty and staff. Flexible work arrangements can also help to balance the demands of work and personal life. Careers at Boise State hr@boisestate.edu (208) 426-1616 960 S Broadway Ave., University Plaza, Ste. 100, Boise, ID 83725-1265 Secondary Navigation myBoiseState Safety, Security and Support Career Opportunities Boise State Public Radio Albertsons Library Transportation and Parking Partner with Us Morrison Center Alumni Bronco Shop and Bookstore University Administration ExtraMile Arena Broncos Logo Athletics Follow us on Facebook Follow us on Instagram Follow us on X Follow us on Youtube Follow us on LinkedIn Terms of Use Accessibility Privacy Classroom Concerns Policies Contact Boise State © 2026 All Rights Reserved\n\t\t\t\t\t\t Boise State University."
